WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.580
Logs need protection because they may contain sensitive operational, user, and security information.

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:11.080
Access should be limited to people and systems with a legitimate need.

3
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:19.100
Developers may need application diagnostics, security teams may need investigation records, and auditors may need specific evidence.

4
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.840
Those needs are real, but they do not require every person to see every field.

5
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:30.500
Role-based access, field-level controls where available, and careful sharing practices reduce exposure.

6
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:36.060
Log integrity means having confidence that records have not been improperly changed or deleted.

7
00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:42.960
Centralized logging helps because application servers do not become the only place records live.

8
00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:54.180
Append-oriented storage, protected audit trails, backups, restricted administrative access, and monitoring of logging pipeline failures all support integrity.

9
00:00:54.280 --> 00:01:00.520
The defensive goal is to make logs reliable enough for operations, investigation, and accountability.

10
00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:07.500
Teams should also monitor whether expected logs are being produced, because a silent logging failure can leave a major evidence gap.

11
00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:10.820
Retention is a policy decision.

12
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:23.160
Logs should be kept long enough for operational troubleshooting, security investigation, legal, contractual, and compliance needs, but not collected or retained forever by default.

13
00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:26.540
Overcollection increases cost and risk.

14
00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:36.080
Encryption in transit and at rest, backup controls, retention schedules, deletion processes, and privacy reviews all belong in the logging design.

15
00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:43.220
A mature program treats logs as sensitive records with lifecycle management, not as a dumping ground.
